WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.620
Governor Bush: Traditional public schools
knock it out of the park in many places.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:06.730
My guess is they'll continue to thrive the
ones that are good.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.150
And when you give parents choices, all schools
get better.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.240
They don't get worse.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.630
They get better.

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:21.199
Nat: Governor Bush, thanks for joining
us here at AEI.

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:22.330
Governor Bush: You bet, Nat.

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:27.480
Nat: So, I wanna sort of get to school
choice in a minute, which is sort of my bailiwick,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.340
but I'm curious to get into a little of your
deep background as governor.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:36.289
When you were governor, you did have big pushes
on school choice, but you also had the A+

00:00:36.289 --> 00:00:42.819
Plan, which had a lot of the hallmarks of,
what we think of as accountability today.

00:00:42.819 --> 00:00:44.260
But back before, it was cool.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.399
So, can you just give me a thumbnail on that
and what do you think were some of the biggest

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:48.620
successes from that early accountability?

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.899
Governor Bush: Well, this will sound like
a bygone era, but when I ran for governor,

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:55.559
I laid out what I wanted to do in education.

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:58.739
I actually laid out the A+ Plan in detail.

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:03.249
It became the prominent dominant issue of
the campaign.

00:01:03.249 --> 00:01:04.720
My opponent attacked me for it.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:05.720
I defended it.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.820
I won, and it gave me a mandate to do it,
which is a lesson that I think policymakers

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.770
and elected officials should remind themselves
of from time to time.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.530
Nat: And you were able to do it fairly
quickly, right?

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.960
Governor Bush: First-year most of it.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.780
So I went to visit 250 schools, kind of dehorn
myself because I've always believed in vouchers,

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.640
which at the time was, you know, very dangerous
apparently.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.950
So the A+ Plan was we would grade schools
based on student learning A, B, C, D, and

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:36.570
F. And if a school was an F, they would be
given a voucher if they were F two years out

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.880
of four.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:44.530
And if they showed improvement, they would
get $100 per student directly to the school,

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:48.380
no cut for the state or the school district.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:54.360
We eliminated social promotion, and the next
year we created the opportunity scholarship

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.539
program, the corporate tax scholarship program,
and the McKay Scholarship Program, which were

00:01:58.539 --> 00:02:04.070
the first statewide voucher programs, kind
of all within a two and three year period.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.069
And it was a big damn deal.

00:02:06.069 --> 00:02:09.190
I mean, it was tumultuous to say the least.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:15.930
And the lessons learned were that you've gotta,
A, fan your priorities first.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:20.349
You can't have provocative policy without
backing it up with money.

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:25.680
Two, you've gotta not just create in terms
of accountability.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.400
You can't just say, "Here are our expectations.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.400
Now go do it."

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.990
You gotta help people that have never actually
been held to account, and so we focused on

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:38.379
struggling schools and providing resources.

00:02:38.379 --> 00:02:42.050
And the system adapted to this in a dramatic
way.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:47.989
Nat: One of my key questions is you
put in a particular pretty toothsome requirement

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:51.709
that if third graders couldn't pass their
reading benchmarks, they weren't going to

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:52.930
fourth grade.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:57.150
And I'm curious about how that works in a
large part because I see in a number of places,

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:03.439
we have really lofty ambitions and instead
of seeing sort of toothsome requirements,

00:03:03.439 --> 00:03:09.000
oftentimes, we see people budding their[SP]
numbers to get, you know, sort of the numbers

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.000
[crosstalk 00:03:08] .

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Governor Bush: You have exceptions that are
Mack truck wide.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.690
Nat: Yeah, exactly, so how would that
go?

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:13.910
That's the first question, how'd that go.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:14.910
It sounds like [crosstalk 00:03:14].

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:20.689
Governor Bush: We went from 32% of students
being level one, which I would call functional

00:03:20.689 --> 00:03:25.390
illiterate, below basic readers, to 14% in
a two year periods.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:32.329
But we had 14% of our kids held back in the
second year, which is, you know, it's a pretty

00:03:32.329 --> 00:03:34.150
provocative idea.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:40.450
But the net result was that...so we created
this gate, and so we're not kidding.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:44.080
We're not gonna have big Mack truck wide exemptions.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.829
But we did summer school again.

00:03:45.829 --> 00:03:50.080
We put reading coaches in every school to
teach teachers how to teach reading.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.250
We gave reading certificates to teachers to
get an additional pay.

00:03:54.250 --> 00:04:00.960
We implemented early childhood education,
all of which, you know, together created improved

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.130
literacy and lower a number of functionally
illiterate kids.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:14.550
It was in my reelection effort where had we
not made those appreciable gains that I described,

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:20.560
had we maintained the status quo, had nothing
changed, it would have been, you know, 25%

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.370
or 30% of all third graders stuck in third
grade.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:24.560
That would have been really ugly.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.710
Nat: You know, since then, there's
been a lot of accountability water under the

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:29.710
bridge.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:30.710
We've had No Child Left Behind.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:33.060
We've had the Obama era waivers, Race to the
Top.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.530
Now we're at ESSA.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:36.670
We've had Common Core.

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:38.780
There's so many things.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:43.910
Knowing what you know now and have learned
about these lessons, what do you wish that

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:47.440
you had known back when you passed A+ that
you didn't know then?

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:53.340
Governor Bush: You know, the A+ Plan worked.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.350
In reality, there were imperfections for sure,
but it worked.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:02.360
So, one of the things that was so helpful
was that we built a database in our state.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:07.000
Researchers use it all the time to kind of
study education outcomes on a longitudinal

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.000
basis.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.840
We were blessed with a data bank that allowed
us to adjust and kind of anticipate where

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:14.100
we would go.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:17.550
So cut scores kind of turned out to be important
in accountability.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:18.550
Nat: Yeah, they do, huh?

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.110
Governor Bush: Yeah, really important.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:24.160
And if you can kind of anticipate where you
go so that you don't have a...you have a bell

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.320
curve instead of everybody's an A or everybody's
an F, that's important.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:36.450
And we had good data to be able to move us
towards, you know, rising student achievement.

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:43.640
Along the way, there was a lot of wavering,
not so much in my administration but after,

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:48.470
you know, there's always a reason not to move
it up a notch.

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:57.050
You know, we've basically I think as a nation,
we had some significant gains, not because

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:00.180
of me, but during my time as governor, many
states had gains.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:05.540
Now we've seen a kind of a flatlining because
there's a push against accountability principally

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:10.830
because testing is, you know, bad, I guess,
from the minds of people.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:14.410
And the reason is that we overtest.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:18.290
There's all sorts of tests that local school
districts require that are part of the statewide

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:20.490
accountability system in these states.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:25.190
And it's almost as long as it's designed to
like end up...You know, we're really hurting

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:27.160
accountability.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:33.320
So our foundation advocates fewer tests, more
relevant tests, and more information from

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.990
those tests to turn it into a diagnostic tool.

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:36.990
Nat: That's interesting.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:45.720
Let me ask you this, sort of juxtaposition
as a bridge to the talk about choice programs.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:52.070
A lot of public school accountability could
be seen as increasing regulation on public

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:53.540
schools.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:57.050
And then, on the other hand, we have private
school choice proposals.

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:01.280
And a lot of the times we say, "You know,
we need a light regulatory touch here."

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.871
How can you explain to people why you can
hold both those things in your hands at the

00:07:04.871 --> 00:07:05.871
same time?

00:07:05.871 --> 00:07:07.300
Governor Bush: I don't think accountability
is regulation.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:14.150
Regulation is the number of, you know, cafeteria
workers you have to have per X number of students

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:20.140
or imposing another mandate on top of schools
because there was an isolated tragic case

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:21.990
of whatever.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:27.430
That's the state mandates that local school
districts are very right to complain about.

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:30.040
Accountability is actually liberating people.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:32.040
You know, we tried to create an environment.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:35.400
Look, we want rising student achievement,
and here's how we're gonna measure it.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:38.960
You go out and do your work and we'll fund
it and we'll help you.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:45.180
So, I would distinguish between traditional
education regulation and accountability.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:50.540
Now, the private...there is a friction in
school choice programs, the private programs

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:56.250
because you don't wanna impose rules on top
of private schools to make them basically

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:58.890
look like traditional public schools.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:00.842
And we've always struggled what to do.

00:08:00.842 --> 00:08:04.350
Do you test if someone takes public money?

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:06.700
Do you disclose it when there's only 10 kids?

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:11.500
You know, there's a lot of details in this
and nuance that gets worked out different

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:12.880
ways by states.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:21.700
But the basic premise is that you're transposing
this top-down bureaucracy and rulemaking process

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:27.230
that's been around for 100 years in the traditional
public schools to parents to make this decision

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:28.230
for themselves.

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:32.099
As long as you give them the right information
and they know how their kid is doing, I think

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:33.849
that that's appropriate.

00:08:33.849 --> 00:08:36.010
Nat: Right.

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:41.240
You support a number of private school choice
programs, all of them and you support charters

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:42.240
as well.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:45.720
I have a lot of folks who really like charter
schools and they say, "You know what, charters

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:46.779
are enough.

00:08:46.779 --> 00:08:47.779
Charters are enough.

00:08:47.779 --> 00:08:50.319
We don't need these private school choice
programs."

00:08:50.319 --> 00:08:51.319
What would you say to them?

00:08:51.319 --> 00:08:52.769
Why would you say we do need private schools?

00:08:52.769 --> 00:08:56.779
Governor Bush: I've met a lot of people that
are, you know, love high-performing charter

00:08:56.779 --> 00:09:01.040
schools in the inner city, and there are some
phenomenal schools that just really hit it

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:05.480
out of the park, but it's not a zero-sum game.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:13.370
Advocating support of archdiocese schools
in New York City does not hurt Success Academy.

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:18.660
It's truly not a question of one or the other.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:22.720
In fact, they live side-by-side, and if they're
both flourishing, all the better.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.800
Nat: So we just had this event upstairs
talking about Education Savings Accounts,

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:29.370
which I've spent a great deal of time studying.

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:33.380
I wanna ask you a couple of questions about
those, but for the viewers that may not know

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:35.450
what they are can you just kind of describe
them?

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:41.250
Governor Bush: Well, they vary, but they're
basically accounts that parents receive.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:45.839
They receive literally an account of money
depending on what's appropriated to be able

00:09:45.839 --> 00:09:51.380
to choose going to a private school or to
break it up, disaggregate it as you said upstairs,

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:52.380
big word.

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:53.380
Nat: Yeah.

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:54.670
We like big words around here.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:56.230
It's the main thing.

00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:58.180
Governor Bush: Hey, I know.

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:03.230
Tutoring, you know, after-school programs,
all sorts of other uses.

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:10.189
In Florida, the principle ESA program that
is really successful is the Gardiner Scholarship

00:10:10.189 --> 00:10:16.960
Program named after the former president of
the state senate who was the sponsor of the

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:17.960
bill.

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:24.449
And now 10,000 parents or students, their
parents get to choose from...they get I think

00:10:24.449 --> 00:10:25.959
up to $10,000.

00:10:25.959 --> 00:10:31.269
So it's a robust amount of money, and it can
be to keep your child in the school, a traditional

00:10:31.269 --> 00:10:33.769
public school but get additional services.

00:10:33.769 --> 00:10:39.670
Those services, by the way, under the federal
law should be provided.

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:44.800
But as you might know, the federal law isn't
always implemented in a faithful way.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:49.720
And a lot of parents are very frustrated that
the public schools don't...their individual

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:53.820
education plan that the law requires be met
isn't being met.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:57.830
So this is a way for them to take that power
and implement it on their own.

00:10:57.830 --> 00:10:58.830
Nat: Sure.

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:02.190
And ESAs, our education savings accounts,
ESAs are sort of the new kid on the block

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:04.300
as far as it comes to choice programs.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:08.950
We have tax credits scholarships, and so I
know we have voucher, traditional voucher

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:10.810
programs.

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:15.829
What is it that they bring that is just totally
different than these other forms?

00:11:15.829 --> 00:11:20.189
Governor Bush: The main difference is that
you're empowering...the money goes to parents

00:11:20.189 --> 00:11:23.750
to choose how they spend it on behalf of their
children.

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:26.390
You know, there's restrictions, of course.

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:32.110
But it's different than a kind of a binary
choice of going to the traditional school

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:35.139
that your kid was already going to or a private
option.

00:11:35.139 --> 00:11:40.740
ESAs give you the choice of going to a private
school, staying in a public school and using

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:42.689
private services along the way.

00:11:42.689 --> 00:11:49.680
So it's a much more dynamic flexible tool
for parents to help their children learn.

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:51.270
Nat: Yeah, that's interesting.

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:53.040
It raises the question of the supply side.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:58.879
We can do a lot about the demand side by giving
families money and ways to use it that they

00:11:58.879 --> 00:12:00.329
otherwise wouldn't.

00:12:00.329 --> 00:12:04.209
But it's sort of harder to develop a supply
side of providers who are gonna kind of meet

00:12:04.209 --> 00:12:05.209
those demands.

00:12:05.209 --> 00:12:08.200
If you just have school choice, well, it's
obvious that schools rise up.

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:11.309
With ESAs, you need all kinds of different
providers.

00:12:11.309 --> 00:12:17.519
What have you seen in Florida and in other
states in your work with the Foundation for

00:12:17.519 --> 00:12:22.930
Excellence in Education that give you the
confidence that the supply side will respond?

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:27.339
Governor Bush: Well, supplies or supply side
always responds to demand.

00:12:27.339 --> 00:12:31.439
I mean, it's kind of how free-market economics
work.

00:12:31.439 --> 00:12:36.800
Unfortunately, this is not a free market in
the sense that if there's, you know, capital

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:41.610
costs or really principal cost for developing
private alternatives.

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:48.889
And there you have to be creative and so if,
you know, you gotta raise capital, you have

00:12:48.889 --> 00:12:55.079
to identify buildings and provide support
for that, it's a challenge.

00:12:55.079 --> 00:13:00.790
But ultimately, what we found was that as
our corporate tax scholarship program, McKay

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:07.230
Scholarship Program, Gardiner Scholarship
Program went from literally zero to now 150,000

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:12.550
students that new schools were formed and
supply ultimately met the need.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:17.800
And there's still big waiting lists of kids
that are trying to get into these programs.

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:18.800
Nat: Right.

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:22.550
Now, a lot of these programs are gonna be
state programs, but they're gonna run into

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:25.769
the school finance structure, which is we
have local dollars.

00:13:25.769 --> 00:13:30.930
I've got some dollars from my state, and across
the nation, there's about 10% federal funding,

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:34.040
45% state, 45% local.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:43.220
Well, if you're state making rules with 45%
of your money to try and provide real educational

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:48.249
opportunities for kids, you're stuck in a
pretty good jam if you can't come up with

00:13:48.249 --> 00:13:53.379
the funds to give people a valuable voucher
or ESA.

00:13:53.379 --> 00:13:55.440
Can you just speak to that challenge?

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.980
Governor Bush: Sure.

00:13:57.980 --> 00:14:04.490
In the case of our programs, the principal,
the largest voucher program is the corporate

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:10.230
tax scholarship program, and it's less per
student than the traditional public school

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:11.230
option.

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:14.689
So, in effect, every child that leaves the
public school to go to the private school

00:14:14.689 --> 00:14:19.220
is saving the state money, and there's a growing
understanding of that which sustains these

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:20.220
programs.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:27.889
So on the one hand, you're...And in Florida,
we have a merging of our local and state funding,

00:14:27.889 --> 00:14:29.640
which just makes this much easier.

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:30.770
Nat: Yeah, it does.

00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:34.589
Governor Bush: So, in the states that don't
have that, you're gonna have to appropriate

00:14:34.589 --> 00:14:35.589
money.

00:14:35.589 --> 00:14:39.889
The Gardiner Program is a separate line item
in appropriation, which gets to the basic

00:14:39.889 --> 00:14:40.889
point.

00:14:40.889 --> 00:14:43.000
If it's a high priority, it ought to be first
funded.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:47.000
You know, Washington doesn't work this way,
but most states if a governor says, "This

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:52.699
is my number one priority," you take the first
monies to fund your first priorities, and

00:14:52.699 --> 00:14:55.420
so we never...we weren't constrained.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:59.189
Other states, you know, where it's a big fight
in the legislature, really are hampered by

00:14:59.189 --> 00:15:00.460
this for sure.

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:06.369
Nat: I'm gonna ask you a question
from your perspective as head of the Foundation

00:15:06.369 --> 00:15:11.600
for Excellence in Education, so can you just
tell our viewers what is the foundation and

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:12.600
what's its mission?

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:13.600
Governor Bush: Sure.

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:15.439
It was formed in 2007.

00:15:15.439 --> 00:15:19.620
It started as a place where we could tell
the Florida story, which is really a story

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:27.999
of choice, accountability, and a focus on
early childhood literacy and the combination

00:15:27.999 --> 00:15:32.559
of all this yielding rising student achievement,
which is ultimately what matters.

00:15:32.559 --> 00:15:41.069
But we've morphed into still telling that
story, and we go state-by-state to policymakers

00:15:41.069 --> 00:15:45.660
in the legislature and in the executive branch
kind of forewarning them of the mistakes that

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:50.600
we might have made, which is always helpful,
not to remake the mistakes that others have

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:56.279
made and encouraging them to be big and bold
and providing support when they do and then

00:15:56.279 --> 00:15:58.559
helping them and guide them on implementation.

00:15:58.559 --> 00:16:07.939
This is complex stuff, so whether it's testing
or just basically the science of reading or

00:16:07.939 --> 00:16:11.440
making sure that accountability system is
done the right way where it's not gonna blow

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:12.869
up in your face the next year.

00:16:12.869 --> 00:16:17.709
We've had states that have ignored our advice
in that regard and it blew up in the next

00:16:17.709 --> 00:16:20.660
year and they had to start over.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:22.170
We wanna be a trusted adviser.

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:25.619
We don't bigdog it, you know, show up at a
place and take credit.

00:16:25.619 --> 00:16:33.709
We operate typically with business, with chambers,
and with local and state-based policy institutes

00:16:33.709 --> 00:16:38.819
to do this in partnership always being deferential
to the leaders that take the...you know, to

00:16:38.819 --> 00:16:42.619
have to do the courageous thing which is to
advocate whether you're a governor or a member

00:16:42.619 --> 00:16:43.619
of the legislature.

00:16:43.619 --> 00:16:44.619
Nat: Sure.

00:16:44.619 --> 00:16:47.680
So taking Florida off the table for obvious
reasons, what other state is just knocking

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:52.100
out of the park in terms of education, generally,
but private school choice programs charters?

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:55.320
Who do you think's really doing great?

00:16:55.320 --> 00:17:02.550
Governor Bush: I'd say Arizona has a robust
charter school program in a burgeoning private

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:03.550
school program.

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:07.240
Governor Ducey is a big and bold leader.

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:11.630
Brian Sandoval did something extraordinary,
but you know the legislature changed and I

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:15.930
don't think he got it off the ground to make
it completely sustainable because there's

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:17.290
not enough scale yet.

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:19.000
But he had a full-throated ESA.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:23.570
Wow, I mean that's the ultimate thing.

00:17:23.570 --> 00:17:26.790
So huge credit for making that big first step.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:35.200
Indiana has...it's kind of followed the footsteps
of Florida and done some things even bigger.

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:41.210
Louisiana, although...you know there's...every
state kind of ebbs and flows.

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:47.650
Tennessee has had big learning gains with
a focus on teacher preparation and public

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:48.650
school choice.

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:50.580
So there are some good states out there.

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:55.100
Colorado is a place where actually bipartisan
consensus has emerged from time to time, is

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:57.080
one of the few states where there's not a...

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:58.150
Nat: That still exist?

00:17:58.150 --> 00:17:59.150
Governor Bush: Yeah.

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:00.150
Well, apparently in Colorado.

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:04.090
It's the water up there or maybe that new
initiative that they've passed [crosstalk

00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:05.090
00:18:02]

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:06.090
Nat: It's the elevation, maybe.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:07.090
I don't know.

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:08.680
Governor Bush: Every state's different.

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:14.710
You know, Mississippi's a place where starting
really from nothing, Governor Bryant's done

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:20.820
a really good job of...they've passed or they're
passing an ESA.

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:25.580
There's big support in the business community
and in the political leadership for meaningful

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:29.240
efforts there, and they're focused on reading.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:31.810
They didn't have a charter school program
until recently.

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:36.110
So depending on where you are on the spectrum
of change, you know, these are big steps.

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:39.770
And we help each state kind of uniquely based
on where they are.

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:43.740
Nat: So, you support vouchers.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:46.540
You support online education.

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:49.820
You support ESAs, charter schools.

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:55.680
And these are all worthy of support, but they
all do inevitably leave students away from

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:58.520
traditional school districts and neighborhood
schools.

00:18:58.520 --> 00:19:01.870
A lot of people will say, "Well, you know,
that's because you hate neighborhood districts

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:03.630
and traditional public schools."

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:08.450
If that's not the case, you know, as these
things grow and they succeed, what do you

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:11.570
see is the future for traditional school districts
and neighborhood schools?

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:15.000
Governor Bush: Well, neighborhood schools
particularly I think are going away because

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:20.900
they have broad support among moms and dads
just because of the convenience and the safety

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:22.410
factor.

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:27.670
Parents have lots of reasons why they support
their children going to particular schools.

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:31.280
It's not always the way the bing counters
and the bureaucrats suggest.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:35.670
And so, traditional public schools will always
be well-funded.

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:41.790
Will there be a little bit of, you know, stress
because the long-term pension obligations,

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:45.970
the sins of the past put pressure on school
districts?

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:48.280
Yeah, but so what, you know.

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:54.410
I mean, if you go to some states, more money's
being spent to deal with the pension obligations

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:56.690
decided 20 years ago.

00:19:56.690 --> 00:20:00.690
Classroom education has languished even though
they spend double what a state like Florida

00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:01.690
spends.

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:05.950
Is that fair, the fact that we were more disciplined
and in how we went about it.

00:20:05.950 --> 00:20:09.730
Should we bemoan the fact that states are
gonna have to make some adjustments?

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:15.460
No, I mean, that's part of what I think is
an important element of a 21st century education

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:20.410
system, to move away from this industrial
model that is failed in the private sector

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:25.730
and would have failed already in the public
sector had it not been for all of us paying

00:20:25.730 --> 00:20:30.070
our taxes to sustain something that's not
as efficient as it needs to be.

00:20:30.070 --> 00:20:34.580
But traditional public schools knock it out
of the park in many places.

00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:38.010
And my guess is they'll continue to thrive
the ones that are good.

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:40.430
And when you give parents choices, all schools
get better.

00:20:40.430 --> 00:20:42.170
They don't get worse, they get better.

00:20:42.170 --> 00:20:45.980
Nat: So, there's a number of private
school or just choice options, right?

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:48.710
There's public school choice and charters.

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:55.000
We have ESA's tax credits scholarships vouchers
and a number of different kinds of programs.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:58.270
So let me present you with a false forced
choice.

00:20:58.270 --> 00:21:04.010
If you had to choose one to bet on, what's
your favorite program of these?

00:21:04.010 --> 00:21:13.260
Governor Bush: I mean, if you created a statewide
ESA program and you created a repository of

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:20.970
options for parents and there was some degree
of transparency about their effectiveness

00:21:20.970 --> 00:21:26.070
and what the price was and all the things
that a consumer of something of that importance

00:21:26.070 --> 00:21:31.300
would need and you had accountability around
it which would be different than the traditional

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:35.420
accountability so that parents would know
what was working and what wasn't.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:36.890
That to me would be the ideal situation.

00:21:36.890 --> 00:21:43.920
I mean, if a girl could dream, it would be
to turn parents, well-informed parents with

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:47.690
accurate transparent information into the
school districts.

00:21:47.690 --> 00:21:49.900
Nat: Yeah, it's a little bit of a
chicken and the egg thing.

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:57.320
Of course, you can't have a vibrant marketplace
of educational service providers without developing

00:21:57.320 --> 00:21:59.740
an information system that gives feedback
to consumers.

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:02.890
And, of course, you can't get that system
up until you have the vibrant system.

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:06.342
But what do you think states should think
about as they're trying to develop that and

00:22:06.342 --> 00:22:08.320
trying to just grease those gears?

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:12.710
Governor Bush: I think that's what they need
to think about is if you pass a voucher bill

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:19.960
or expanded charter, any expansion of parental,
the parents are ultimately accountable for

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:24.390
their children's education you've gotta along
the way, at the same time, simultaneously

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:27.430
give them a system of real information.

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:31.840
You know, as I mentioned upstairs, that's
a place where generally the governments have

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:33.140
failed.

00:22:33.140 --> 00:22:34.230
They just don't think that way.

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:35.580
They are insular in their nature.

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:36.680
They've never had to market.

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:41.360
You know, they never had to actually learn
how to present something in a way that a mom

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:44.240
and a dad would understand how they're doing.

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:49.320
In fact, in some cases, they don't want you
to know because, you know, if kids aren't

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:50.720
learning, it's not like a great conversation.

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:52.890
Nat: That's true, that's true.

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:54.190
Keep it quiet.

00:22:54.190 --> 00:22:55.190
Indeed.

00:22:55.190 --> 00:22:59.530
Governor Bush: Well, how you get to the place
where, you know, you have someone graduating

00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:04.940
with a high school diploma with an 8th-grade
level reading capability.

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:10.520
How do you get to the point where a significant
percentage, 40, 50% of kids are college and/or

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:11.520
career ready?

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:14.630
More than that actually they have to retake...if
they go to college, they have to retake high

00:23:14.630 --> 00:23:16.010
school reading and high school math.

00:23:16.010 --> 00:23:19.910
The only way you do it is that you've kept
it quiet because no mom would say, "Well,

00:23:19.910 --> 00:23:20.910
that's great.

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:25.210
My little Johnny went 12 years and he's a
9th-grade reader, woohoo."

00:23:25.210 --> 00:23:27.110
Nat: Yeah, not a lot to cheer about.

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:31.860
Let me ask you about advice for legislators
or governors that are trying to develop an

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:32.920
ESA in their state.

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:39.540
Now, of course, these are gonna go through
the push and pull of making legislation sausage-making,

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:43.600
and they have to compromise, sometimes on
price, sometimes on how many people are eligible,

00:23:43.600 --> 00:23:47.670
sometimes on what kind of regulation they're
gonna cover.

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:52.450
Where would you say if you're developing an
ESA bill you can't compromise on?

00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:54.060
Where can they not afford to compromise on?

00:23:54.060 --> 00:24:01.010
Governor Bush: I think it's making sure that
there's going to be private providers opting

00:24:01.010 --> 00:24:03.250
in, which means price.

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:10.300
It would be better to cap the number and go
back to fight again the next session of raising

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:17.160
the cap but making sure that this amount of
money while lower than the per student public

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:20.090
school it always should be lower than that.

00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:24.870
But it's enough to be able to attract high-quality
providers into the arena.

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:28.470
Nat: And so why would you say it should
always be lower quality?

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:33.200
Not lower quality, a lower amount of money
then what is spent in the local public schools

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:34.431
as I just following up on that.

00:24:34.431 --> 00:24:35.431
Governor Bush: Why?

00:24:35.431 --> 00:24:39.890
Because any transformation, you know, in life
outside of government innovation normally

00:24:39.890 --> 00:24:42.280
cost less and yields a better result.

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:49.190
And we should apply that model I think rather
than the tidal model that cost more money.

00:24:49.190 --> 00:24:55.320
More money isn't the leading indicator of
a better result and plus politically, if you're

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:59.400
gonna go propose...I got this great idea,
I'm gonna propose a voucher that is double

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:04.780
the cost of 50% more or 25% more than the
per-student funding, by the way, because it's

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:07.730
private, we're not gonna impose all these
rules on top of them.

00:25:07.730 --> 00:25:11.260
So, you know, we don't know exactly if it's
gonna be perfect.

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:14.230
You're not gonna get that passed in the state
legislature.

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:20.170
Nat: A number of people that I know
or I'm familiar with, they will criticize

00:25:20.170 --> 00:25:25.370
private school choice programs on equity grounds.

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:29.130
They will say, "Well, you know, this is...the
people who are gonna take advantage of this

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:33.530
are disproportionately white or rich or something
else."

00:25:33.530 --> 00:25:35.920
Governor Bush: The private school choice programs?

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:36.920
Nat: Yes.

00:25:36.920 --> 00:25:39.120
And so how do you respond when you hear arguments
like that?

00:25:39.120 --> 00:25:40.730
Governor Bush: It's just not true.

00:25:40.730 --> 00:25:41.910
It's not true at all.

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:43.920
Every data point suggests otherwise.

00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:49.060
Most of these programs are taken advantage
of by lower-income people.

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:55.400
The programs for children with learning disabilities
are pretty, you know, across the spectrum,

00:25:55.400 --> 00:26:01.330
both higher income and lower income, but there's
no disproportionate that I'm aware of whether

00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:05.510
it's charter schools or private voucher programs.

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:10.940
The exact opposite is true that people, because
of how the programs are typically crafted,

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:14.470
people of lower-income benefit from these
programs.

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:20.770
The issue that I think is important is to
make sure that parents that are...you know,

00:26:20.770 --> 00:26:27.340
a single mom that's struggling with two jobs
and sees this as a chance for their child

00:26:27.340 --> 00:26:32.020
to rise up and to be successful to go to a
private school that they're informed, they

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:35.670
get the proper information, you know.

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:37.210
That to me is a bigger issue.

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:41.251
If you're in a more affluent area and you
have the information available and you can

00:26:41.251 --> 00:26:47.420
seek it out and, you know, you have a network
of community that can just kind of by the

00:26:47.420 --> 00:26:50.760
tom-toms tell you what teacher is good, what
school is good.

00:26:50.760 --> 00:26:54.800
If you don't have all that, then I think it's
the obligation of the state to provide that

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:57.960
information to level out the playing field.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:04.280
Nat: So private school choice has
a friend in the White House and at the Department

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:05.660
of Education that they never really had.

00:27:05.660 --> 00:27:11.820
You know, charters had support from the administration
before but not private school choice.

00:27:11.820 --> 00:27:16.150
But private school choice has grown in state
capital after state capital, it has not been

00:27:16.150 --> 00:27:18.930
Washington DC that's been pushing it.

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:23.270
So do you think that this new support from
the administration is gonna be a change in

00:27:23.270 --> 00:27:31.340
that and how might states, you know, make
good on that new support in DC?

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:36.480
Governor Bush: Well, it could be that there's
a...there'd be a federal voucher program emerge

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:43.490
in the welfare reform, the fight against poverty
that emerges in Paul Ryan's world next...this

00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:44.490
year.

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:45.490
I don't know.

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:51.270
That would be wonderful to include it because
empowering parents that are near at the poverty

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:56.510
level with more choices in education would
be a key element of that.

00:27:56.510 --> 00:28:02.570
I'm a little wary of it only in that it should
not hurt the existing choice programs that

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:04.340
exist across the country.

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:05.650
It should enhance them.

00:28:05.650 --> 00:28:09.760
There should be enough flexibility that if
the federal government wants to provide support

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:14.070
which states that already have these voucher
programs and school choice programs would

00:28:14.070 --> 00:28:18.280
love to have, it shouldn't be with so many
strings attached that it stifles the work

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:20.370
that's already being done.

00:28:20.370 --> 00:28:26.330
So my guess is that we'll continue on this
path that this is really a state issue locally

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:27.420
administered.

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:33.810
The federal government has...with Betsy DeVos,
certainly is a strong advocate of accelerating

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:36.920
school choice programs across the country
and hopefully, she could be a partner.

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:45.010
But I cannot imagine a time when the Trump
administration with Secretary DeVos' input

00:28:45.010 --> 00:28:49.270
at least would make it harder for the existing
choice programs to prevail.

00:28:49.270 --> 00:28:52.470
Nat: Jeb Bush, thanks for coming to
AEI and talk about us.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:53.600
Governor Bush: You bet.

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:54.600
Thanks.

00:28:54.600 --> 00:28:57.420
Nat: Hey, everyone, that's the end
of our discussion with Governor Jeb Bush.

00:28:57.420 --> 00:28:58.470
Thanks for watching.

00:28:58.470 --> 00:29:03.520
As always, let us know what other topics you'd
like AEI scholars to cover on Viewpoint.

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:07.550
And to learn more about Education Savings
Accounts, check the links in the description

00:29:07.550 --> 00:29:08.050
below.

